# dc01.tf.todo - YOUR turn. This is the file you write.
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Read web-srv.tf until you understand every line.
#   2. Fill in the blanks below (the <<< ... >>> markers).
#   3. Rename it to dc01.tf   (Terraform only reads files ending in .tf, so while
#      it's .tf.todo it is ignored - that's why your half-finished work can't
#      break anything).
#        mv dc01.tf.todo dc01.tf
#   4. Run `terraform fmt` then `terraform validate` to catch syntax mistakes,
#      then `terraform plan` to see what it WOULD build. Nothing is created until
#      you `apply`.
#   5. Compare with answers/dc01.tf only after you've tried. (answers/ is a
#      subfolder, so Terraform never reads it.)
#
# dc01 is a WINDOWS VM (it becomes the domain controller), so it's closer to
# win-srv in core.tf than to web-srv for the VM block - but it has NO public IP,
# like web-srv. It's gated by var.enable_dc01, with [0] indexing, same as web-srv.
#
# Predict first: dc01 holds every company account. Should it ever have a public
# IP? How will you reach it to administer it? (Write your answer in a comment.)

